  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "A chart titled 'Which vaccines can be given today?', with the line 'It is better to vaccinate late than never!'. Rows are ages (birth, 6 weeks, 10 weeks, 14 weeks, 9 months, 18 months, 2 years); coloured columns are vaccines, each starting at the age of its first dose and running downward as an arrow. HepB BD and BCG start at birth, HepB BD ending at a stop sign; OPV 1-3, PCV 1-3, Penta 1-3 and RV 1-3 at 6, 10 and 14 weeks, RV ending at a stop sign before 2 years; IPV at 14 weeks; MCV 1 at 9 months; DTP4 (or Penta4), MCV 2 and MenA at 18 months. A 'When to give' column gives the timing for each row (for example, at 10 weeks or as soon as possible thereafter, and at least 4 weeks after dose 1), and a 'When not to give' column gives the limits: HepB BD not after 6 weeks; RV1, RV2 and RV3 not after 2 years of age; later doses not before 4 weeks since the previous dose; MCV1 and MenA not before 9 months of age except where indicated. A footer says there is no need to restart a series after a long gap, and there is no upper age limit for most vaccines except rotavirus (under 2 years) and the hepatitis B birth dose (under 6 weeks).",
